# Injectivity of cusp-to-cusp integration

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.integration_cochain_injective`
(`4517a847-79d3-42b1-8f1e-a22121966e2c`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `5749c8d5-7af2-4241-99af-635becec394b`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N>0$ and $k\ge 2$, and let

$$
I:S_k(\Gamma_1(N))\longrightarrow H_c(N,k-2;\mathbf C)
$$

be the complex-linear map whose underlying modular symbol is cusp-to-cusp integration, normalized by
$-2\pi i$. Then $I$ is injective: a cusp form whose integrals between every pair of rational cusps
vanish is identically zero.

This is the analytic injectivity assertion in the modular-symbol realization of the Eichler–Shimura
map.

## Explanation of the source proof

We reduce injectivity of the integration map to the sibling problem `MTT.period_vanishing`, which
asserts that a cusp form all of whose normalised vertical integrals $2\pi\int_0^\infty
f(r+it)(r+it)^j\,dt$ ($j\le k-2$, $r\in\mathbf Q$) vanish is zero. That statement is the assumption
of this submission; everything else is algebra.

**Proof.** Let $I$ be linear with $(If)$ given by the integration cochain
$\Phi_f(x,y)=P_f(y)-P_f(x)$, where $P_f(r)$ is the cusp period polynomial (its coefficient of
$X^jY^{k-2-j}$ is $\binom{k-2}{j}$ times the modular integral, `coeff_cuspPeriodPolynomial`) and
$P_f(\infty)=0$. Suppose $I f=I g$. By linearity $I(f-g)=0$, hence $\Phi_{f-g}=0$, and evaluating at
$(\infty,r)$ gives $P_{f-g}(r)=0$ for every $r\in\mathbf Q$ (`integrationCochain_infty`). Reading
off the coefficient of $X^jY^{k-2-j}$ for $j\le k-2$ yields $\binom{k-2}{j}\cdot 2\pi\int_0^\infty
(f-g)(r+it)(r+it)^j\,dt=0$, and since the binomial coefficient is non-zero all modular integrals of
$f-g$ vanish. By `period_vanishing`, $f-g=0$, i.e. $f=g$.

